Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:02.550 align:start position:0%
 
germany's chancellor angela merkel is  preparing to visit regions of germany  affected by severe flooding that has  killed at least 156 people  merkel is due to visit one of two  regions hit by the floods  the disaster area in germany spans more  than a hundred kilometers  near the borders with the netherlands  and belgium where the district of  arvada was the worst hit a massive  cleanup operation is underway  as rescue crews search for survivors  more than a thousand people are still  missing dw reporter kate brady has this  report from some of the flooded villages  around  bonn  destruction and debris on every corner  the only sound on the streets mud being  scraped away  and tractors rumbling through the once  picturesque town  of arvila here in avila the  water has subsided and it's only now  that the real true  extent of the damage caused by the  floods is really coming to light a lot  of people are returning  to their homes for the first time since  the floods the scenes are devastating  for those returning  the owners of this home moved in only  six months ago the flood water  reached halfway up the walls of the  first floor  we couldn't react quickly enough to move  our belongings  we had to sit upstairs for 24 hours  because the fire service came and asked  have you been hurt if not then you'll  have to manage on your own  but at least we're alive  i'm living there are so many dead  this is it's unbelievable you can't  imagine what's happened here  volunteers family and friends have  traveled far and wide to arvala to help  those affected  they're here clearing homes or supplying  people with the basics  this morning i was handing out breakfast  down by the river there was a family  that couldn't come out of their house  so i got the supplies to them over the  balcony via the garage roof  um  many locals right now want financial  help from the state  quickly and with as little red tape as  possible  further north in efstadt a donation bank  at a school has been  overwhelmed with clothes and food  the donations have stopped now because  they just kept coming it just became too  much  there's definitely plenty of willingness  to help  some locals evacuated from afstad this  week are also being sheltered at the  school  many feel left in the dark  there should be an official from the  city that you can talk to here even if  they don't have much new to say  but at least then you'd have the feeling  that they care let's go  for dimension come to commences we don't  know whether we'll get in our house  again at all  can you even live there or is it all  gone forever  for many of those affected that question  could take weeks  months if not years to be answered  and dw reporter julia saudelli is on the  ground for us in  schuld a town of 660 inhabitants uh  julia how's the situation there  just a few days after the flooding  yeah where we're standing now we're in  the upper part of schulte of the town  and behind me one can see the  devastation  and all the destruction that the water  has brought through the town some of the  houses that were closer to the river  have been  completely swept away houses further  back further away from the water are  destroyed up to the second floor  some are completely gutted filled with  mud and debris it's really a dramatic  situation and it's not only the  destruction of things  the death toll is has been growing still  the police in the area have said that  only in the availa area here where we  are now  they have counted up to 110 deaths and  they expect the death toll to  continue increasing and they are  currently searching the area  with helicopters to try and recover any  victims  julia german chancellor merkel is  expected to visit the area  where you are today i'm going to come  back to you in a moment but we have some  reaction from people dealing with this  disaster there first  i think it's very important that our  political leaders show up here  on one hand to express personal sympathy  but also to get a picture of the  situation on the ground themselves  and then if necessary to promise help  financial help or government help  via the bundeswehr or via the technical  relief agency  if they bring shovels buckets and brooms  and lend a hand  i think it's fine that they come i'm  already here but if they are just coming  here to gawk  i don't think that's okay then they  should please stay away  and angela merkel the german chancellor  as we said  earlier is going to be visiting that  town where you are now  you've been talking to people what kind  of reception is she going to get  some people are quite skeptical of her  visit they think it is just a photo  opportunity  during the election campaign and are not  really convinced about her visit  while others think it's a good idea it's  an important show  of solidarity and support but what  everyone here is telling me is that they  want this  support from the government to continue  even after the next few days and weeks  when the  immediate rescue operations and cleanup  operations are going to happen  people here know that it's going to take  years properly to  to build their towns back up again and  they want the government to be there and  support them  okay julia saudelli in schultz thank you  so much for that  and for more insight on the floods and  how they may affect both medical and  politics and the upcoming federal  election campaign i'm joined now by our  political correspondent emmanuel  thanks for being here um the merkel's  um let's say the party of merkel's  christian democratic union  has as its front runner armin lashett he  could be the next chancellor he was as  we just said in the headlines  seen smiling during a somber speech by  the german president about the floods  explain this controversy yes yesterday  the german president frank walter  steinmeier was presenting his  condolences to the people  affected he was talking about how  heartbreaking the situation  was not only for uh the people affected  but also for  the entire country for uh for germany  and then in the background you had the  frontrunner candidate for the  conservatives amin lachet who also  happens to be the state premier  in northrop westfalia the worst affected  region  uh together with reinan uh about  palatinate he was seen laughing in the  background  so even though he promptly apologized  and said that his attitude was  inappropriate and that he wasn't  laughing at the situation  well this really irritated to say the  least a lot of people  the video got a million view within  hours  yesterday and this could be quite  damaging for his  for his campaign ahead of the election  uh and  would that perception of emotional  disconnect really  significantly hurt the christian  democratic union's chances  the whole party's chances you think well  you know it's not the first controversy  since thursday he already was  seen as being quite susceptible when  journalists  asked him about his environmental track  record in northwind  westphalia and he said no wasn't the  time and a place for political debate  yet he's a politician so when  then can you expect a debate on the  question of climate change which  uh which really is becoming more and  more pressing also  uh you know his track record in  northround westphalia is not  very positive when it comes to  environment for example he allowed  coal mines to run up until 2038  even if he came back on those  declarations uh  in may you know he's now the candidate  for germany  for to become a german chancellor so uh  he  you know he said that this uh phase out  of  coal uh mines of coal energy could  happen uh quicker but he is not known  as being a climate you know you know an  environmental  uh policies maker  and but it looks like the environment is  being  um the global warming i should say is  being attributed as a cause  of the flood or contributing by almost  all of the politicians  in germany and since we're two months  and a few days next to  the next federal election is it possible  that this flooding and the tragedy in  germany will affect the outcome  definitely and how not to think about  what happened almost  two decades ago when gerhard schroeder  who was then chancellor and was running  for a second  mandate uh revealed himself as the  christian councillor the chancellor  of crisis when similar floods happened  uh  uh in dresden more than 30 000 people  had to be evacuated and  on the ground you could see schroder who  until then hadn't really led a very  successful  re-election campaign and who had a very  positive uh track record  during his mandate at chancellor well he  really revealed himself as the  crisis chancellor and that's something  that many were  waiting to see i mean lashed doing and  he hasn't quite  risen to the occasion just yet and that  could really cost him  a lot of votes in september okay two  months to go we'll see how it all plays  out  against the backdrop of this tragedy  thank you emmanuel  while some german towns begin to assess  the damage and clean up others further  south are being hit by new flooding  in the southeast in saxony rail lines  are closed due to rising waters  a state of emergency has been declared  in parts of the state of bavaria  across the border there in austria  flooding and heavy rains are causing  serious damage  in the town of helene close the german  border a flash flood  swept through the streets people living  there had already been warned to be  careful  and to avoid going into their cellars  and basements  other parts of europe has also been  badly affected by the floods  tens of thousands of people have been  evacuated from southern parts of the  netherlands  and belgium is also counting the cost at  least 27 people died there  the region around the moose river was  the worst affected by what authorities  are calling an  unprecedented deluge of water  the floods have receded from the streets  of pepinster  leaving the mud behind residents are  toiling to reclaim their homes  praying that the rain holds off  days of heavy downpours turned this  gentle stream  into a raging torrent houses along the  riverbank  overwhelmed by the force of the water  cars swept off the roads  residents in shock  we've never seen anything like this  before i would never have imagined it  there's been a bit of water before but  nothing like this  we could never have imagined the water  up to the ceiling it's a catastrophe  european commission president ursula von  des line came to take stock of the  damage  and she's promising long-term help  of course the european union has been  helping  with helicopters with boats but i just  reassured the citizens that we will of  course be here also for reconstruction  outside assistance is welcome but the  community is pulling together  to get through the worst  i'm really surprised but very happy to  see the solidarity that has developed  honestly it's amazing  the water is on its way out but it will  be a long time before things get  back to normal

